= Momentum — Technical =

== Definition ==
For a classical particle of constant mass:

<pre>
p = m v
F_net = dp/dt
</pre>

Momentum p and velocity v are vectors. The impulse J of net force over an interval equals the change in momentum, Δp, under Newtonian mechanics.

== System accounting ==
For a set of interacting particles, total momentum is the vector sum of the individual momenta. Internal Newton-third-law forces cancel in the sum; the external impulse determines the change in total momentum. Apparent violations can result from ignoring expelled mass or forces across a poorly specified [[System boundary|system boundary]].

== Momentum versus kinetic energy ==
Classical kinetic energy K = (1/2)m|v|² is a nonnegative scalar; it can change in an inelastic collision even when momentum is conserved in an isolated system. The low-speed formula is not an adequate relativistic momentum definition.

